<p>The same <code>CLAUDE.md</code> has a different scope depending on where you put it.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Enterprise (managed)</strong> &mdash; applies to every member inside the company.</li>
    <li><strong>User global</strong> &mdash; always applies on my PC, whatever project I'm working on.</li>
    <li><strong>Project</strong> &mdash; applies only in that project (shared with teammates).</li>
    <li><strong>Project local</strong> &mdash; instructions inside the project that are <strong>not shared, just for my own PC</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Practice &mdash; creating instructions</h3>
<p>Run <code>claude</code> in a new folder, then ask for this to create a <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.</p>
<pre><code>Create a CLAUDE.md file in this folder.
Instructions: always write answers in Korean,
and always append _new to the names of new files you create.</code></pre>
<p>Once the file is created, the instructions you just gave ("write in Korean", "append _new to new files") are recorded inside it. Every later task <strong>always follows these instructions underneath this folder.</strong> For example, if you say "save a short poem as a text file", the result is written in Korean and the filename carries <code>_new</code>.</p>

<h3>What is worth putting in</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Rules that keep original files from being modified</li>
    <li>Rules to follow when writing code (Python, for example)</li>
    <li>Output format &middot; how files are saved</li>
    <li>Tone &middot; language of what gets written</li>
</ul>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Tip</span>
    You can have the AI create <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, or open and edit it yourself. On Windows, line breaks in a prompt may not work well, but writing it <strong>all on one line without breaks</strong> is perfectly fine.
</div>
